SumsOfSquares -- A package for sums-of-squares problems
*******************************************************

Description
===========

SumsOfSquares is a package to solve sums-of-squares (SOS) problems via *note
semidefinite programming: (SemidefiniteProgramming)Top, (SDP).

Introduction

Writing a polynomial as a sum of squares proves its nonnegativity for all
arguments, but not all nonnegative polynomials are sum of squares. While
nonnegativity of a polynomial is hard to check, there are efficient methods to
find sums-of-squares decompositions, and this package makes some of them
available in Macaulay2. These methods rely on semidefinite programming, an area
from mathematical optimization. Several solvers (or tools) for semidefinite
programming are available. The package *note SemidefiniteProgramming:
(SemidefiniteProgramming)Top, allows us to use some of these solvers in
Macaulay2. The solver CSDP (see https://github.com/coin-or/csdp/wiki ) is
included in Macaulay2 and can be used without any configuration.  See *note
Solver: (SemidefiniteProgramming)Solver, for how to use other solvers.

Usage examples

The most basic application is to (try to) decompose a polynomial as a sum of
squares using the function *note solveSOS: solveSOS,.
+---------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y];                            |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i2 : f = 2*x^4 + 2*x^3*y - 2*x^2*y^2 + 5*y^4;|
+---------------------------------------------+
|i3 : sol = solveSOS f;                       |
+---------------------------------------------+
The return value is an object of type *note SDPResult: SDPResult, which, in the
case of success, contains the sum-of-squares decomposition. It can be extracted
with *note sosPoly: sosPoly,.  This returns an object of type *note SOSPoly:
SOSPoly,, which supports many operations that polynomials support.
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : s = sosPoly sol                                       |
|                                                           |
|            83 2    2 2    43  20 2       2    231773   2 2|
|o4 = (5)(- ---x  + y )  + (--)(--x  + x*y)  + (------)(x ) |
|           200             20  43              344000      |
|                                                           |
|o4 : SOSPoly                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
The command *note value(SOSPoly): value_lpSOSPoly_rp, can be used to check that
the decomposition found matches the original polynomial.
+-----------------------------+
|i5 : value(s)                |
|                             |
|       4     3      2 2     4|
|o5 = 2x  + 2x y - 2x y  + 5y |
|                             |
|o5 : R                       |
+-----------------------------+

Sums of squares modulo equality constraints

The package supports sum-of-squares *note problems in quotient rings:
solveSOS_lpRingElement_cmRingElement_cmMatrix_rp,. This can be useful to prove
nonnegativity of a polynomial on a variety.  The following example is taken
from [P05].  Consider the problem of proving that the polynomial f = 10-x^2-y
is nonnegative on the circle defined by g = x^2 + y^2 - 1. To do this we check
if f is a sum of squares in the quotient ring modulo g. For such a computation,
a degree bound must be given by the user.
+------------------------------------------+
|i6 : R = QQ[x,y];                         |
+------------------------------------------+
|i7 : S = R/ideal(x^2 + y^2 - 1);          |
+------------------------------------------+
|i8 : f = 10-x^2-y;                        |
+------------------------------------------+
|i9 : sol = solveSOS (f, 2);               |
+------------------------------------------+
|i10 : sosPoly sol                         |
|                                          |
|       79       4 2    71    2     63    2|
|o10 = (--)(y - --)  + (--)(x)  + (---)(1) |
|        8      79       8         632     |
|                                          |
|o10 : SOSPoly                             |
+------------------------------------------+
The previous computation gives decomposition with three sums of squares. See
*note TraceObj: TraceObj, for how to obtain a decomposition with only two
squares.

Other cool stuff

  * The package implements Hilbert's algorithm to decompose a nonnegative
    ternary form into a sum of squares of rational functions: *note
    sosdecTernary: sosdecTernary,.
  * Sums of squares problems can be solved parametrically: *note solveSOS:
    solveSOS,.
  * Optimization over varieties can run using *note lowerBound: lowerBound,.

On the role of the coefficient field

The SumsOfSquares package interfaces tries to hide some of the difficulties
that arise from using these numerical procedures. See *note coefficient field:
coefficient field, for more information.

