solveSOS(RingElement,RingElement,Matrix) -- sum-of-squares problem in a quotient ring
*************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note solveSOS: solveSOS,
  * Usage: 
        solveSOS(f,mon)
        solveSOS(f,objFun,mon)
        solveSOS(f,D)
        solveSOS(f,objFun,D)
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, a polynomial
      * objFun, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, a linear
        function of the parameters (optional)
      * mon, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, a vector of monomials
        (alternatively a degree bound D)
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note RoundTol: RoundTol, => ..., default value 3, tolerance for
        rational rounding
      * *note Solver: solveSOS_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSolver=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, picking a semidefinite programming solver
      * *note TraceObj: TraceObj, => ..., default value false, whether to use
        trace as the objective function
      * *note Verbosity: solveSOS_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVerbosity=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value 0, control the level of information printed
  * Outputs:
      * an instance of the type *note SDPResult: SDPResult,, 

Description
===========

This method allows to compute sums-of-square decompositions in quotient rings.
A vector of monomials must be provided in this case.

+----------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y]/ideal(x^2 + y^2 - 1);              |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : f = 10-x^2-y;                                  |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : mon = matrix {{1}, {x}, {y}};                  |
|                                                    |
|             3      1                               |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R                                |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : solveSOS (f, mon)                              |
|                                                    |
|o4 = MomentMatrix   3x3 matrix over RR_53           |
|     GramMatrix     3x3 matrix over QQ              |
|     Monomials      3x1 matrix over R               |
|     Status         SDP solved, primal-dual feasible|
|                                                    |
|o4 : SDPResult                                      |
+----------------------------------------------------+

If a degree bound $D$ is given, the method will use the vector of monomials of
degree at most $D/2$.

+----------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : solveSOS (f, 2)                                |
|                                                    |
|o5 = MomentMatrix   3x3 matrix over RR_53           |
|     GramMatrix     3x3 matrix over QQ              |
|     Monomials      3x1 matrix over R               |
|     Status         SDP solved, primal-dual feasible|
|                                                    |
|o5 : SDPResult                                      |
+----------------------------------------------------+

Parametrized sums-of-squares problems can also be solved in quotient rings.

+----------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : S = R[t];                                      |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : solveSOS(f-t,-t,mon,RoundTol=>12)              |
|                                                    |
|o7 = MomentMatrix   3x3 matrix over RR_53           |
|     GramMatrix     3x3 matrix over QQ              |
|     Monomials      3x1 matrix over R               |
|     Parameters     1x1 matrix over QQ              |
|     Status         SDP solved, primal-dual feasible|
|                                                    |
|o7 : SDPResult                                      |
+----------------------------------------------------+

Caveat

The *note rational rounding: coefficient field, may also fail in quotient ring
computations. In this case, the object *note SOSPoly: SOSPoly, constructed from
the object *note SDPResult: SDPResult, can live in a newly created ring,
instead of the quotient that one started with.

+------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : R = QQ[x,y]/ideal(x^2 + y^2 - 1);          |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : f = 10-x^2-y;                              |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : mon = matrix {{1}, {x}, {y}};             |
|                                                |
|              3      1                          |
|o10 : Matrix R  <-- R                           |
+------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : s = solveSOS (f, mon, RoundTol=>infinity);|
+------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : ring sosPoly s                            |
|                                                |
|o12 = RR  [x..y]                                |
|        53                                      |
|                                                |
|o12 : PolynomialRing                            |
+------------------------------------------------+

The above ring is not a quotient ring. One can construct a new quotient ring
and work there. However, this will only work reliably for principal ideals, as
otherwise the Gröbner basis engine might fail due to inexact computations.

+----------------------------------------------+
|i13 : R' = ring sosPoly s;                    |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i14 : S = R'/(sub (ideal (x^2 + y^2 - 1), R'))|
|                                              |
|o14 = S                                       |
|                                              |
|o14 : QuotientRing                            |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i15 : sub (f, S) == sub (sosPoly s, S)        |
|                                              |
|o15 = false                                   |
+----------------------------------------------+


See also
========

  * *note solveSOS: solveSOS, -- solve a sum-of-squares problem

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * "solveSOS(RingElement,Matrix)"
  * *note solveSOS(RingElement,RingElement,Matrix):
    solveSOS_lpRingElement_cmRingElement_cmMatrix_rp, -- sum-of-squares problem
    in a quotient ring
  * "solveSOS(RingElement,RingElement,ZZ)"
  * "solveSOS(RingElement,ZZ)"
