sosdecTernary -- sum of squares decomposition for ternary forms
***************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        (p,q) = sosdecTernary(f)
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, a homogeneous
        polynomial in 3 variables
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note RoundTol: RoundTol, => ..., default value 3, tolerance for
        rational rounding
      * *note Solver: solveSOS_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSolver=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value "CSDP", picking a semidefinite programming solver
      * *note Verbosity: solveSOS_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVerbosity=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value 0, control the level of information printed
  * Outputs:
      * p, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of *note SOSPoly: SOSPoly,s
      * q, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of *note SOSPoly: SOSPoly,s

Description
===========

Given a nonnegative ternary form $f$, this method uses Hilbert's algorithm to
compute a decomposition of $f$ as $f=\frac{\prod_ip_i}{\prod_iq_i}$ where each
factor $p_i$ and $q_i$ is a sum of squares. The method returns null if $f$ is
not nonnegative. As an example, consider the homogeneous Motzkin polynomial.

+--------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = RR[x,y,z];                   |
+--------------------------------------+
|i2 : f = library ("Motzkin", {x,y,z});|
+--------------------------------------+
|i3 : (p,q) = sosdecTernary (f);       |
+--------------------------------------+

The result, in this case, is a quotient of two sums of squares.

+-------------------------------------------+
|i4 : (#p, #q)                              |
|                                           |
|o4 = (1, 1)                                |
|                                           |
|o4 : Sequence                              |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i5 : clean(1e-4, f * value q#0 - value p#0)|
|                                           |
|o5 = 0                                     |
|                                           |
|o5 : R                                     |
+-------------------------------------------+

References: Products of positive forms, linear matrix inequalities, and Hilbert
17th problem for ternary forms, E. de Klerk, and D.V. Pasechnik, European J.
Oper. Res. (2004), pp. 39-45.

Caveat
======

$\bullet$ This implementation only works with the *note solvers:
(SemidefiniteProgramming)Solver, "CSDP" and "MOSEK".

$\bullet$ Due to the iterative nature of the algorithm, it could happen that
some of the output sums of squares are rational while some are real.

Ways to use sosdecTernary:
==========================

  * "sosdecTernary(RingElement)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note sosdecTernary: sosdecTernary, is a *note method function with
options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

