sosPoly -- make an SOS polynomial
*********************************

  * Usage: 
        s = sosPoly (SDPR)
        s = sosPoly (mon,Q)
        s = sosPoly (R,polys,coeffs)
  * Inputs:
      * SDPR, an instance of the type *note SDPResult: SDPResult,, the result
        of an SDP computation
      * Q, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, positive semidefinite (Gram
        matrix)
      * mon, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, a vector of monomials
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, 
      * polys, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of polynomials
      * coeffs, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of scalars
  * Outputs:
      * s, an instance of the type *note SOSPoly: SOSPoly,, 

Description
===========

This method creates an object of type *note SOSPoly: SOSPoly,. Very often this
is applied to an object of type *note SDPResult: SDPResult,, the result of a
semidefinite programming computation.

+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y];                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : f = 2*x^4+5*y^4-2*x^2*y^2+2*x^3*y;                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : sosPoly solveSOS f                                    |
|                                                           |
|            83 2    2 2    43  20 2       2    231773   2 2|
|o3 = (5)(- ---x  + y )  + (--)(--x  + x*y)  + (------)(x ) |
|           200             20  43              344000      |
|                                                           |
|o3 : SOSPoly                                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+

One can also input a Gram matrix $Q$ and a vector of monomials $mon$.

+----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : Q = matrix(QQ,{{1,1,1},{1,1,1},{1,1,1}});|
|                                              |
|              3       3                       |
|o4 : Matrix QQ  <-- QQ                        |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i5 : mon = matrix{{1},{x},{y}};               |
|                                              |
|             3      1                         |
|o5 : Matrix R  <-- R                          |
+----------------------------------------------+
|i6 : sosPoly(mon,Q)                           |
|                                              |
|                   2                          |
|o6 = (1)(x + y + 1)                           |
|                                              |
|o6 : SOSPoly                                  |
+----------------------------------------------+

Alternatively, a sum-of-squares polynomial can be created from a list of
generators and weights.

+------------------------------------+
|i7 : s = sosPoly(R, {x+1,y}, {2,3} )|
|                                    |
|              2        2            |
|o7 = 2*(x + 1)  + 3*(y)             |
|                                    |
|o7 : SOSPoly                        |
+------------------------------------+


See also
========

  * *note solveSOS: solveSOS, -- solve a sum-of-squares problem
  * *note Solver: (SemidefiniteProgramming)Solver, -- picking a semidefinite
    programming solver

Ways to use sosPoly:
====================

  * "sosPoly(List,List)"
  * "sosPoly(Matrix,Matrix)"
  * "sosPoly(Ring,List,List)"
  * "sosPoly(SDPResult)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note sosPoly: sosPoly, is a *note method function:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

